    {
      "id": "legal_texas_rental_laws",
      "filename": "texas_rental_laws.txt",
      "content": "TEXAS RENTAL PROPERTY REGULATIONS\n\nSTATE RENTAL LAWS:\n- No statewide rent control or rent stabilization laws\n- Landlords may increase rent with proper notice (30 days for month-to-month)\n- Security deposits limited to reasonable amount (typically 1-2 months rent)\n- No maximum rent increase restrictions\n\nSHORT-TERM RENTAL REGULATIONS:\n- State preemption prevents local STR bans in most areas\n- Local municipalities may regulate through zoning and permitting\n- Transient occupancy taxes vary by city (typically 6-17%)\n- Austin: 90-day limit per year for unhosted STRs\n- Dallas: No specific STR restrictions at city level\n- Houston: Permits required for STR operations\n\nEVICTION PROCESS:\n- 3-day notice to pay or quit for non-payment\n- 30-day notice for month-to-month tenancies\n- No just-cause eviction requirements\n- Landlord may enter property with 24-hour notice\n\nPROPERTY TAXES:\n- No state income tax - relies heavily on property taxes\n- Average property tax rate: 1.8% of assessed value\n- Homestead exemption reduces taxable value by $40,000\n- Senior citizens (65+) receive additional $10,000 exemption\n\nZONING AND DEVELOPMENT:\n- Generally landlord-friendly regulations\n- Few restrictions on property modifications\n- Accessory dwelling units (ADUs) allowed in most residential zones\n- Mixed-use development encouraged in urban areas\n\nINSURANCE REQUIREMENTS:\n- Landlord liability insurance recommended\n- Flood insurance required in designated flood zones\n- Windstorm insurance required in coastal counties\n- No mandatory tenant insurance requirements\n\nHOA REGULATIONS:\n- HOAs have broad authority to set rental restrictions\n- May limit number of rental units in development\n- Can impose minimum lease terms (typically 6-12 months)\n- Architectural review committees control property modifications\n",
